  "slug": "harbingers-full-collection",
  "title": "Harbingers — Full Collection",
  "summary": "Technoscientific innovations have long animated imaginaries of cyborgs, mutants, and automatons. As our environment becomes synthetic and computationally governed, what lifeforms will emerge? Harbingers assembles a posthuman bestiary, exploring transhumanist desires and technogenic anxieties in an age of accelerated biosocial transformation. Mutant creatures emerge from computational manipulation, genetic engineering, and cross-species contamination, blurring nature, culture, and technology. These organisms—coded chimeras, GM superhumans, algorithmic captives, and waste-metabolizing plants/animals—serve as sentinels of technology's transfigurative powers and ecocide.\n\nWith mutation, mutability, and human-machine entanglement as principles, Harbingers examines art's circulation within blockchain-enabled cultural production. Reflecting blockchain's environmental impact and financial speculation—which, as Alexander Iadarola <a href=\"https://outland.art/perpetual-futures/\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"link\">puts it</a>, has \"leaked into everything\"—works leverage blockchain data to \"infect\" each NFT. Upon purchase, each subsequent blockchain sale triggers a random glitching process, progressively distorting image and audio.\n\nThis intervention highlights digital media's materiality, emphasizing maintenance and stewardship in digital art preservation, and questioning blockchain's \"immutability\" as works change with each transaction. It asks us to consider blockchain's radical transformations in virtual and physical domains.\n\nA harbinger is a premonition, a future haunting, a signal anticipating events or an instigator creating their possibility. This ambiguity of co-production is central to speculation: imagining futures and betting on their becoming real. The show asks us to heed today's signals of transformation and consider what futures we're betting on.",
